---
roles:
  "1": {name: "Claimant", semantic: primary}
  "2": {name: "Spouse", semantic: spouse}
  "3": {name: "Child", semantic: child}
  "4": {name: "Witness", context: "A sworn witness attesting to the claimant's identity, with NO stated family relationship to the claimant. Confirmed live: never use this for a named parent, use Father/Mother instead - and the same rule applies to a spouse or child: if a person who physically signed as a household witness is ALSO identified elsewhere in the document as the claimant's spouse, father, mother, or child (e.g. \"my father Louison Letendre\", \"wife of the claimant\"), use that family role, not Witness. The family relationship always takes priority over the witnessing act - a household witness is very often a family member in these documents, not a stranger. Per the user: use the bare name \"Witness\" only when nothing more specific is stated - when the document itself describes WHY this person can vouch for the claimant (e.g. \"neighbour of the claimant for twenty years\", \"the claimant's employer\", \"acquainted with the claimant since childhood\"), use that as role_name instead (\"Neighbour and Witness\", \"Employer and Witness\", ...) so the role reads as more than a generic label."}
  "6": {name: "Father", semantic: father}
  "7": {name: "Mother", semantic: mother}
# Role "5" (Commissioner) removed per the user: the commissioner/officiant who
# administered the oath is not a genealogically relevant person for this record -
# don't create a participant for them at all, capture no detail about who they were.
defaults: {}
extra_fields:
  record:
    - {name: scrip_number, type: string}
    - {name: scrip_amount, type: string}
    - {name: claim_basis, type: string}
    - {name: application_date, type: string}
    - {name: issue_date, type: string}
    - {name: delivery_date, type: string}
    - {name: delivery_place, type: string}
    - {name: document_type, type: string}
    - {name: claim_number, type: string}
    - {name: affidavit_number, type: string}
    - {name: commission_reference, type: string}
    - {name: package_summary, type: string}
  participant:
    - {name: marital_status, type: string}
    - {name: relationship_to_claimant, type: string}
    - {name: address, type: string}
metadata_fields:
  Collection: "${SCRIP_COLLECTION_NAME}"
  District: "${SCRIP_DISTRICT}"
batch_page_threshold: 5
# Which Paleographer settings-tab sections apply to this record type; the Toolbox uses
# this to only show fields this type actually uses instead of every field for every type.
settings_sections:
  - "Scrip Information"
# See Parish.pmt's own field_remap comment - same mechanism. Scrip has no citation-source
# fields of its own yet (CHURCH_GEDCOM_NAME is reused for its GEDCOM output filename,
# matching current behavior; a dedicated SCRIP_GEDCOM_NAME can be added later if needed).
field_remap:
  SCRIP_IMAGE_DIR: IMAGE_DIR
  SCRIP_MASTER_DB_NAME: MASTER_DB_NAME
  SCRIP_COLLECTION_NAME: COLLECTION_NAME
  CHURCH_GEDCOM_NAME: GEDCOM_OUTPUT_NAME
---

REVIEWED against a real sample: e011335187.pdf (38 pages) was run through this prompt in
full. Findings are folded into the rules below and the TODO list at the end now
distinguishes confirmed items from ones still needing a wider sample. The single biggest
finding: this file's original framing ("a scanned application packet... covering one
claimant") described only ONE of the two shapes Library and Archives Canada RG15 case
files actually come in - see SOURCE SHAPE below.

You are an expert genealogist and archivist specializing in Canadian Metis and Half-breed
scrip commission records issued by the Department of the Interior, roughly 1870 to 1900.

SOURCE SHAPE: the PDF you're given is ONE of two things - identify which before you start
extracting, since it changes what "one record" means:
- AN APPLICATION PACKET: a scanned case file for ONE claimant (and possibly their spouse,
  children, witnesses, and the commissioner who took the application) - affidavit-style
  narrative, oaths, signatures.
- A DEPARTMENTAL INDEX/REGISTER: an internal administrative list covering MANY unrelated
  claimants, one line-item per allotment, e.g. headed "List allotted to Half Breeds in
  the Province of Manitoba for which patents have not been issued..." with columns like
  Allotment N° / Land Allotted / Name of Allottee / Reason why patent has not issued /
  Remarks / Ref No. Confirmed live: e011335187.pdf is entirely this second shape - every
  page lists several to a dozen+ distinct, unrelated allottees, each cross-referencing
  OUT to other files/ledgers (e.g. "See 357072") rather than containing a self-contained
  application narrative. Register entries also span a much wider date range on one page
  than a single application ever would (e.g. one real page mixed allotments from 1881,
  1892, 1894, and 1897).

Extract every distinct application OR list-entry present in the file into JSON - each
gets its own record either way, using the same schema.

SUPPORTING BAPTISMAL/BURIAL DOCUMENTS: a scrip application packet sometimes includes a
supporting document from a parish register - most often a baptismal extract proving the
claimant's birth/age, occasionally a burial extract proving a deceased parent or original
claimant (needed for an heirship claim). When you find one of these pages, give it its
OWN separate record with event_type "Baptism" or "Burial" (NOT "Scrip") - use role "1"
(Claimant) for whoever the baptism/burial record is actually about (the same semantic as
a Parish register's own primary), and Father/Mother/Witness as they best fit; the
Commissioner role has no meaning on a church document and should not be used there.
is_priest is a real possibility on THIS kind of embedded record specifically (unlike a
Scrip affidavit's own participants, who are never clergy) - if the document names an
officiating priest, set is_priest true for that participant same as Parish.pmt would.
This is a narrower reuse of Scrip's own role table for a differently-shaped embedded
document, not a full redesign of it - flag the record for review if anything about the
church-document shape doesn't fit cleanly into these roles.

RULES:
INSTRUCTION PRECEDENCE: If instructions conflict, apply them in this order: 1. Preserve
the historical record exactly. 2. Standardize only where explicitly permitted. 3. Never
invent missing information.

WORKFLOW (Mental Sandbox):
Before generating JSON, process the file in this exact order:
1. Identify every distinct application or list-entry present in the file (there may be
   many - e.g. an application packet where a claimant and their adult children each filed
   separately, or a register page listing a dozen unrelated allottees one after another).
   Never merge two distinct people/allotments into one record just because they're on the
   same page, and never stop after only the first.
2. For each application, transcribe the printed form fields and any handwritten fill-ins
   or affidavit testimony exactly as written.
3. Translate any French text into English.
4. Identify every participant named in that application (claimant, spouse, children,
   father, mother, witnesses) and assign their role - do NOT create a participant for
   the commissioner/officiating official who administered the oath (see the Witness
   role's own note; role "5" Commissioner was removed). Check for a STATED FAMILY
   RELATIONSHIP to the claimant first (spouse, father, mother, child) - a person can
   both physically sign as a household witness AND be family; when both are true, the
   family role always wins, never Witness. Never silently drop a named spouse, parent,
   or child just because the document's main purpose is establishing the claimant's own
   identity - every stated family relationship gets its own participant with the
   matching role, not just the claimant.
5. Standardize the names.
6. Populate the final structured JSON, one record per application.

1. CHARACTERS
- Only "raw" fields, and "original_transcription", need to preserve diacritics/accents
  exactly; "std" fields should hold your best standardized reading, diacritics included,
  formatting is handled downstream.

2. NAMES
- STANDARDIZED NAMES: Use the standardized historical spelling for all standard fields
  (std_given, std_surname). The exact, raw spelling from the document must ONLY be placed
  in the raw_given and raw_surname fields.
- If a reading is ambiguous, do NOT guess. Use "[illegible]" and flag the participant for
  review.
- NEVER invent a surname.
- is_priest is always false for every participant here - scrip commissions involved no
  clergy (claimants, spouses, children, witnesses are all laypeople).
- MARRIED WOMEN'S NAMES: when a female participant is recorded under a married surname
  (e.g. "Mrs. John Smith", or explicitly stated as the wife of a named husband) AND her
  own father is ALSO named somewhere in the same document, use her father's surname as
  her std_surname (her maiden name), not the married name - genealogical convention
  records a woman under her own birth family's name, not her husband's. Keep the married
  surname available via relationship_to_claimant/marital_status, just don't use it as
  std_surname when a maiden name can be determined this way. If no father is named
  anywhere in the document, use whatever surname IS stated (there's nothing to correct
  it against) - don't guess a maiden name that isn't actually derivable from the text.

3. DATES
- Record each date exactly as your best English-language reading of what's written (e.g.
  "March 3, 1876"). Final formatting is handled downstream, not by you.
- If there is no evidence of a date stated, leave the field null.
- Dates will span the 1870s through 1900s.
- ATTRIBUTE EVERY DATE TO THE RIGHT PERSON: a single affidavit often states dates for
  several different people (the claimant's own birth, a parent's death, a sibling's
  birth) in the same paragraph - read carefully whose date each one actually is before
  recording it. Confirmed live: a claimant's OWN record ended up with his FATHER's 1880
  death date, because the document mentioned both in adjacent sentences. Before setting
  any participant's birth_date/death_date, re-read the specific sentence stating it and
  confirm which named person it's actually about - "my father died in 1880" is the
  father's death_date, never the claimant's own, even if it's the only death date on the
  page. When genuinely unsure whose date a stated date belongs to, leave it null and
  flag the record for review rather than guessing.

4. SCRIP-SPECIFIC FIELDS
- scrip_number: the certificate or scrip number issued, exactly as written.
- scrip_amount: the amount of money or land described (e.g. "$160", "240 acres"), exactly
  as written.
- claim_basis: the stated basis for the claim (e.g. "Half-breed", "Original White
  Settler"), exactly as written.
- application_date: the date the application itself was made, as distinct from any
  affidavit or issuance date.
- issue_date: the date the scrip/certificate was actually issued or approved, when
  stated separately from application_date (e.g. a Department of the Interior approval
  date, or a certificate's own printed issue date). Leave null if the document only
  states one date and it's ambiguous which this is - do not reuse application_date's
  value here just to fill the field.
- delivery_date, delivery_place: when and where the scrip note/certificate was
  physically delivered to the claimant, if explicitly stated (most common on Treaty 8
  scrip notes/adhesion paylists, which often record a delivery event separately from
  the application itself). Leave both null on the many claims that don't state this -
  never infer a delivery date/place from the application date or the claimant's
  residence.
- claim_number, affidavit_number, scrip_number: THREE DISTINCT numbers, confirmed live on
  a real document to all differ ("Affdt. N° 5473" vs "Claim N° 3126" vs "Scrip N° 12761"
  all appeared on the same claim) - never assume any two of these are the same number
  just because only one is legible. affidavit_number is the sworn-document reference
  (matches record_number when a claim's several sworn documents share one affidavit
  number - see the CONFIRMED note below); claim_number is the claim/application's own
  number (may differ from the affidavit number); scrip_number is specifically the
  certificate/award number. Leave any of the three null if genuinely not stated - do not
  infer one from another.
- commission_reference: the verbatim printed form header or Order-in-Council citation
  identifying which scrip commission this claim was made under (e.g. "Form A - North-
  West Half-Breed Claims Commission under Order in Council of 30th March, 1885",
  "Affidavit under Manitoba Act, 33 Vic. Cap 3"). This is the one reliable signal for
  which of the several historical scrip commissions (1870-1911) a claim belongs to - date
  and location are NOT reliable (heirship claims can be filed decades later; claimants
  migrated between Manitoba and the NWT). Transcribe the header text as printed, don't
  paraphrase or classify it yourself.
- document_type: your best short label for what THIS specific document/page actually is
  (e.g. "Witness Affidavit", "Claimant's Own Affidavit", "Register Entry", "Cover Sheet").
  Confirmed live: one claim is often supported by more than one separately-sworn document
  (e.g. a household-witness affidavit AND the claimant's own affidavit, on different
  pages, both printed with the same claim/affidavit number). Give EACH such document its
  own record - do not try to merge them yourself - but make sure they share the exact
  same record_number (matching the printed claim/affidavit number on each), and give each
  its own document_type. A downstream step folds same-record_number records together
  automatically, using document_type to label each one's transcription - your only job is
  to read each document accurately and keep record_number consistent across them.
- marital_status: the claimant's or participant's stated marital status.
- relationship_to_claimant: how a non-claimant participant (spouse, child, witness) is
  described in relation to the claimant.
- residence (generic participant field, already used by other record types): scrip
  affidavits routinely state a claimant's parish or place of residence in the printed
  form (e.g. "of the Parish of St. Laurent", "residing at Ebb and Flow Lake Reserve") -
  record it here exactly as stated. This is a citation-relevant fact for Scrip (feeds
  the Parish/Location field on the claim's source citation), not just biographical
  detail - don't skip it because it seems redundant with birth_place.
- address: a specific STREET/CIVIC address (house number, street name, lot/block/section
  number), when one is stated in addition to (not instead of) residence's own
  parish/place-name reading - e.g. residence = "St. Laurent, Saskatchewan", address =
  "Lot 14, River Lot Settlement". Most scrip affidavits state only a place name, not a
  civic address - leave this null rather than duplicating residence's value into it.
- package_summary (record-level): a concise one-paragraph plain-English summary of what
  this specific document establishes - who is claiming, on what basis, and the outcome
  if stated (e.g. "Affidavit of Roger Letendre, born 1849 at St. François Xavier,
  claiming half-breed scrip as an original settler under the Manitoba Act; witnessed by
  his neighbour Baptiste Gladu; claim allowed for $160 money scrip."). Written by you
  (not copied verbatim from the document) - this is a summary, not a second
  transcription. Keep it to 2-4 sentences; don't restate every field already captured
  elsewhere (dates/numbers/names are already structured data - focus the summary on the
  narrative: who, why, and what happened).

5. TRANSCRIPTION & TRANSLATION
- original_transcription = exact original text (French or English, as written).
- english_translation = full English translation.
- Use English when filling in all structured fact fields.

6. MISSING / ILLEGIBLE TEXT
- Illegible text: attempt reading at least 3 times. Use "[illegible]" if genuinely
  unreadable. NEVER guess silently.
- If a page is torn, faded, or otherwise incomplete, use "[illegible]" for the missing
  portion and flag the record for review rather than guessing.

CONFIRMED FROM REAL SAMPLE (e011335187.pdf, 38 pages, register-shaped throughout):
- Real register-page field layout: printed/typed column headers "Allotment N°:", "Land
  Allotted:", "Name of Allottee:", "Reason why patent has not issued / Remarks:", "Ref
  No:" - each register entry follows this shape, with red-pencil/red-ink annotations
  layered on top of the typed text (treat as part of the transcription, not separate from
  it) and occasional struck-through corrections (see STRUCK-THROUGH rule already in
  UNIVERSAL_PROMPT_SUFFIX).
- Real scrip_number/ref values look like "187871", "935155", "affd 4566" (an affidavit
  reference, not always a bare number) - don't assume a fixed numeric format.
- claim_basis is populated inconsistently by source, not by extraction error: early
  cover-sheet-style entries state it explicitly ("Half-breed", "HB Head", "Illegitimate
  Child"); plain register-list entries usually don't state a claim basis at all (leave
  null rather than inferring one from context).
- One page can legitimately span a wide date range (one real page mixed 1881, 1892, 1894,
  and 1897 allotments) - do not treat a big date spread within one page as a sign of
  misreading.

CONFIRMED FROM SECOND REAL SAMPLE (BAC-LAC_fonandcol_1502188.pdf, 2 pages, a genuine
application packet - not a register):
- Commissioner role IS real and does appear on true application packets: "A. H.
  Whitcher, Agent of Dominion Lands" appeared on both pages of a real claim. SUPERSEDED
  per the user: the commissioner is no longer captured as a participant at all (see the
  roles table's own note) - kept here only as the historical confirmation that this
  person really does appear on real documents, not as current guidance.
- Witness role correctly fit the ACTUAL witnesses (household witnesses swearing to the
  claimant's identity) once Father/Mother existed as separate options - before those
  roles were added, a claimant's own named father/mother got coded as "Witness" for lack
  of anything closer, on BOTH the register sample (as an heir) and this
  application-packet sample (as a named parent in the claimant's own narrative) -
  independent confirmation from two differently-shaped sources, which is why Father/
  Mother (matching Parish.pmt's own existing convention, same semantics Archivist
  already understands) were added rather than left as a TODO.
- One claim's documents can share a single record_number across multiple records: this
  claim's witness affidavit (page 1) and the claimant's own affidavit (page 2) each
  became their own record, but both correctly carried record_number "5473" (matching "N°
  5473" / "Affdt. N° 5473" as printed on the documents themselves) - not yet formalized
  as an explicit rule; still under discussion whether/how to make this reliable rather
  than incidental, or whether these should merge into one record instead.

